 "observations": [
  "The page number «147» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the right the number of the table, «Tab. 36», in bold sans serif, underlined. At the foot, the signature «19*» on the right; recorded here, not transcribed as a line. The titles (L01-L03), in bold, the sigla in roman; L03, the place of Jupiter's node, in smaller bold type, «2 S. 22 G. 1 M.», with note 1. The folios of the manuscripts stand at the right of the last title in two lines, «C 60v, O 118r,» and «N 13r.», the letters raised; here as right margin notes.",
  "The table: rules and heads as on pp. 144-146 (JP2 0176-0178), with «Pagina numerorum (O) Viae numerorum», the second line without a siglum, over the four columns of the arguments, divided below a rule into «Augmentum2) proprietatum omnium» and «Diminutio proprietatum omnium» in smaller type; «Sublimatio Jovis examinata3)», without «ascensus»; «Examinatio ipsius elheza4)» and «Examinatio centri», each hyphenated «Exami-natio»; «Statio prima Jovis»; «Latitudo Jovis descendentis5)» over «prima» and «secunda» in smaller bold type. Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L04-L08), the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. L09, the units, in bold.",
  "L10-L39. Checked as on p. 144 (JP2 0176): the Sublimatio is 5s 22° 32′ less half the Examinatio argumenti in every row, a half minute counted as a whole. The Latitudo secunda is 2° 30′ · sin x to within 1″ in every row, and the value for 90° + y is that for 90° − y on p. 146 (JP2 0178) in every row except at 114°: there the edition prints O's 2° 17′ 1″ (note g), where C's 2 agrees with the computation, 2° 17′ 1.9″, and with the 2° 17′ 2″ of p. 146 at 66°.",
  "L10-L39. The Examinatio argumenti reaches its greatest value, 10° 52′, from 98° to 103°; an epicycle of radius 11.3 gives a greatest equation of arc sin (11.3 : 60) = 10° 51.3′, at 100.9°. Against the equation of that epicycle (pp. 144-146) the table is 0.5′ to 2.3′ larger in every row. The Examinatio centri for 90° + y is that for 90° − y on pp. 145-146 (JP2 0177-0178) except at 98°, 99°, 101° and from 115° to 119°, where it is 1′ smaller, and from 107° to 110°, where it is 1′ larger.",
  "L10-L39, the other columns: the Statio prima increases from 4s 5° 43′ to 4s 6° 24′ by 1′ or 2′ a degree. The Latitudo prima is 1° 17′ to 94° and decreases by 1′ every fourth degree, to 1° 10′ at 119° and 120°; like that of Saturn (p. 141, JP2 0173), it is not symmetric about 90° and continues to decrease from the values of p. 146.",
  "Notes on the readings. Note 1: the title of the node, 2s 22° 1′, is N's; O has none, and C has 2. 12. 1, the reading that p. 145 (JP2 0177, L02) prints from O. Note 3: C's «Aug elmusteri muadala», the Arabic awj al-mushtarī al-muʿaddal, the corrected apogee of Jupiter, Suter's «Apogeum ... aequatum» for the Sublimatio examinata (p. 49, JP2 0081, L17-L18). Note a: the last argument, 4s 0°, is N's; C and O have 3. Notes b-b and c-c: C leaves out the degrees, 4, of the Examinatio centri from 100° to 120°, and the degree, 2, of the Latitudo secunda from 109° to 119°.",
  "Notes on the readings, continued. Checked: the Latitudo secunda 2° 19′ 5″ at 112° (note d, N, against C's 18 and O's 29), 2° 19′ 4.7″ computed; 2° 29′ 38″ at 94° (note e, O), 2° 29′ 38.1″ computed; 2° 28′ 32″ at 98° (note f), 2° 28′ 32.4″ computed, where C and O have 52 and N's reading is not reported.",
  "N04: the range mark «b)—b)» has a dash printed in two pieces, 17 and 13 px long with a gap of 5 px, together the length of the other dashes (35 px); transcribed as one long dash, as in «c)—c)». Italics: «om.». N01: the ellipsis «. . . .» of the lemma as printed. N04: «2) ON» and «om. C. —»: see the critical notes."
 ],
